<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/expert/econometrics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the methodological summit of the economics ladder. You have already met the
    questions it arms you for: <em>GDP and National Accounts</em> taught you what output is and
    how it is measured, <em>Fiscal Policy and Public Debt</em> and <em>Monetary Policy and
    Central Banks</em> gave you the policy levers whose effects we now want to measure, and
    <em>Unemployment and Labour Markets</em> supplied the labour regularities this chapter
    estimates. Econometrics is the discipline of measuring those effects honestly when you
    cannot run the experiment yourself.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the evaluation problem and explain why before-and-after and treated-versus-untreated comparisons both fail;</li>
    <li>compute a difference-in-differences estimate from a two-group, two-period comparison and name the assumption it stands on;</li>
    <li>read multiplier, VAR and Taylor-rule evidence critically and say what each identification strategy assumes;</li>
    <li>explain spurious regression, the Solow residual and the Phillips curve's instability as lessons about what regression can and cannot deliver;</li>
    <li>apply the credibility revolution's standard: design beats statistics, and robustness is a culture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Econometrics is statistics under the constraints economics actually imposes: you
    rarely choose your treatment, the data is observational, and the questions are
    causal — did that policy work, by how much, for whom? Its baseline tool is OLS
    regression, with its assumptions stated openly (linearity, exogeneity, no perfect
    collinearity); everything else in the field is a repair for where those assumptions
    break, above all exogeneity, which is exactly what policy evaluation doubts. This
    chapter runs the econometrician's toolkit across the unit's macro agenda —
    government intervention, fiscal policy, monetary policy, supply-side policy, growth,
    unemployment and inflation — because each of those topics is also a case study in
    one methodological lesson. The recurring moral, from the credibility revolution that
    won the 2021 Nobel: design beats statistics, and a clean natural experiment with
    simple methods beats a clever model on messy data.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the identification problem and the toolkit that tames it" widget="equationStep" config={ecomCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel walks the field's core argument in four steps.
    Step 1 states the identification problem — every estimate is a mix of the cause you want and
    the causes you do not; step 2 lists the repairs; step 3 argues that a natural experiment
    beats a clever model; step 4 is the professional culture that checks every repair. Reveal
    the steps one at a time and try to predict the next one before pressing on — the prediction
    is the test that the structure has stuck.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> econometrics is OLS plus honesty — a baseline regression whose
  assumptions, above all exogeneity, are exactly where policy questions bite. Everything else
  in the field is repair work, and the first repair is learning what a causal answer demands.</p>

  <h2>Identifying what policy actually did</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>evaluation problem</strong>: after a policy, we observe the economy with
    the policy — never the same economy without it. A naive before-and-after comparison
    fails because everything else was changing too (recessions end, oil prices move); a
    comparison with unaffected regions fails if affected and unaffected were already
    trending apart. The workhorses of repair are <strong>panel data</strong> — many units
    tracked over many periods, with fixed effects absorbing every time-invariant
    difference between them (a state's culture, a firm's management) — and
    <strong>difference-in-differences</strong>, which compares the <em>change</em> in an
    affected group to the change in an unaffected one. Identification quality is then
    checked, not asserted: did the two groups move in parallel before the policy?
    None of these repairs is free — each buys identification by assuming something
    specific (parallel trends, no spillovers, correct timing), and the craft is making
    those assumptions visible and checkable rather than implicit.
    Increasingly, macro policy is also identified at high frequency — comparing asset
    prices in a half-hour window around a central-bank announcement, where nothing else
    plausibly moved — a strategy that trades scope for cleanliness.
  </p>
  <p>
    Difference-in-differences in miniature: a jobs-programme region's employment moves
    from 100 to 104, while an untreated comparison region moves from 100 to 99. The
    before-and-after view credits the programme with 4; the treated-versus-untreated
    snapshot credits it with 5; the difference-in-differences estimate is
    (+4) − (−1) = +5, because the control region's decline reveals what the treated
    region would have done anyway. Two numbers in, one counterfactual out — the entire
    method on a napkin, and every refinement (matching, synthetic controls, event-study
    plots) is an argument that this comparison is the right one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "statistical significance means the effect is big."
    Significance combines size with sample variance; with administrative data covering
    millions, a trivially small coefficient is "significant" weekly. Applied
    econometrics reads the coefficient and its units first, the asterisk second.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the evaluation problem has a toolkit — panel data with fixed
  effects, difference-in-differences, high-frequency event windows — and every tool buys
  identification only by assuming something checkable. Now apply the toolkit to the field's
  flagship number: the fiscal multiplier.</p>

  <h2>Fiscal policy: estimating the multiplier</h2>
  <p>
    The fiscal multiplier — output change per unit of government spending or tax change —
    is the field's flagship estimation problem, because governments do not randomise
    budgets. The measurement approaches each carry a lesson. Narrative identification
    (Romer and Romer, 2010) reads presidential speeches and legislative records to
    isolate tax changes motivated by long-run philosophy rather than the current
    economy, and finds output falls by roughly 2–3% per 1% of GDP in tax increases.
    Cross-state comparisons (spending spilling over borders) return multipliers around
    1.5. And the crisis gave the field its self-audit: Blanchard and Leigh (2013) showed
    that when forecasters assumed multipliers near 0.5 while cutting budgets in
    2010–2011, growth disappointed by roughly 0.9–1.7 percentage points per 1% of GDP of
    consolidation — the assumed multipliers were too small, exactly as a
    slack-economy model predicts. Multiplier estimates vary with the monetary response,
    openness and slack; that variation is the finding, not noise.
  </p>

  <h2>Monetary policy: rules, VARs and long lags</h2>
  <p>
    Central banks move one short rate; the economy's response must be estimated.
    <strong>Vector autoregressions</strong> (Sims, 1980) model GDP, inflation and
    interest rates as a joint system whose past drives its present; a shock to the rate
    equation then traces an impulse response — typically output dips with a hump over
    roughly a year, inflation follows later. Because the series are trending
    (non-stationary), VARs work with differences or must respect unit roots; the
    textbook caution is the spurious regression (below). On the policy side, the
    reference point is the Taylor rule (1993): set the policy rate to
    2 + inflation + 0.5 × (inflation − 2) + 0.5 × output gap — a description that became
    a benchmark. Worked example: inflation 3%, output on trend → prescribed rate
    2 + 3 + 0.5 × 1 = 5.5%. The lag structure — Friedman's "long and variable" — is
    itself an estimated object, and the reason policy evaluation needs a model rather
    than a glance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "two trending series that regress well are related."
    Regress one random walk on another and you routinely get high R² and large
    t-statistics from pure coincidence — Granger and Newbold's spurious regression
    (1974). With unit roots you need differencing, or cointegration: the special case
    (Engle–Granger, 1987) where two trending series genuinely move together, like short
    and long interest rates.
  </p>

  <h2>Supply-side policy: slow effects, harder identification</h2>
  <p>
    Supply-side policy — tax wedges, labour-market reform, competition, education —
    operates through incentives on hours, investment and productivity, over years
    rather than quarters. That timescale makes evaluation harder: over a decade, too
    much else changes for before-and-after logic. The dominant econometric strategy is
    the cross-country growth regression (Barro's tradition): regress growth on initial
    income and controls, and read off <strong>conditional convergence</strong> —
    estimates cluster around 2% per year, meaning a country half as rich as its own
    steady-state benchmark closes half the gap in roughly 35 years (0.98<sup>35</sup> ≈
    0.49). The standing critique is that policy choices are endogenous — governments
    reform when in crisis, so reforms ride the very shocks they are blamed or credited
    for — which is why the best supply-side evidence comes from sharp, datable reforms
    evaluated with difference-in-differences and a pre-trend check, not from sweeping
    regressions alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Economic growth: growth accounting</h2>
  <p>
    The most honest decomposition in macroeconomics is arithmetic. With capital's share
    α ≈ 0.3, growth accounting writes output growth as
    ΔY/Y = α × ΔK/K + (1 − α) × ΔL/L + ΔA/A, where ΔA/A — the Solow residual — is
    productivity growth: everything output growth cannot be traced to capital or labour.
    Worked example: output grows 3.2%; labour hours grow 1.4% (contributing
    0.7 × 1.4 = 1.0 points); the capital stock grows 3.0% (contributing
    0.3 × 3.0 = 0.9 points). The residual is 3.2 − 1.0 − 0.9 = 1.3 percentage points of
    productivity. The residual is often called a measure of our ignorance — it bundles
    technology, efficiency, institutions and measurement error — and that honesty is
    the point: growth accounting tells you precisely how much of the growth story your
    data actually explains, and how much is attribution. Cross-country versions add a
    sobering layer: capital shares, hours and price levels are measured differently in
    different statistical offices, so residual comparisons between rich and poor
    countries compare measurement regimes as much as economies.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> growth accounting decomposes output growth and leaves the Solow
  residual — a measure of our ignorance — as an honest remainder. The same honesty is needed for
  the labour-market regularities, where a rule of thumb and a famous natural experiment carry
  the rest of the chapter.</p>

  <h2>Employment and unemployment: Okun's law and the minimum wage</h2>
  <p>
    Two estimable regularities anchor the topic. Okun's law (1962): each percentage
    point of unemployment above its natural rate costs roughly 2% of output relative to
    potential — unemployment at 8% against a 5% natural rate implies an output gap of
    about −6%. The law is a rule of thumb, not a mechanism: it compresses participation,
    hours and productivity into two numbers, and its coefficient drifts across decades.
    And the minimum-wage literature became econometrics' showcase. Card and
    Krueger (1994): New Jersey raised its minimum from 4.25 to 5.05 dollars an hour in
    April 1992 while neighbouring Pennsylvania stayed at 4.25; surveys of about 410
    fast-food
    restaurants gave full-time-equivalent employment per store of 20.44 to 21.03 in New
    Jersey and 23.33 to 21.17 in Pennsylvania — a difference-in-differences of about
    +2.76 jobs per store in the treated state, the opposite of the competitive textbook
    prediction. A later comparison using payroll records confirmed the pattern. Getting
    such comparisons right also demands honest inference machinery:
    heteroskedasticity-robust and <strong>clustered</strong> standard errors (allowing
    shocks to hit all observations in a state at once) — because mis-measured standard
    errors are the most common technical failure in applied work.
  </p>

  <h2>Inflation: the Phillips curve as cautionary tale</h2>
  <p>
    Phillips (1958) fitted a smooth curve to UK wage inflation against unemployment over
    1861–1957, and for a decade policy was designed around the trade-off it seemed to
    offer. Then the 1970s produced high inflation <em>and</em> high unemployment
    together, exactly as Friedman (1968) and Phelps (1967) had argued: the curve is not
    a menu, because expectations adjust — squeeze unemployment below its natural rate
    and inflation ratchets rather than employment settling higher. The estimated object
    that survived is the expectations-augmented curve, plus the
    <strong>sacrifice ratio</strong> — output lost per point of disinflation. The
    Volcker episode is the worked case: US inflation of about 13.5% in 1980 was cut to
    roughly 3% by 1983, at the price of unemployment peaking at 10.8% in 1982 and a
    deep recession. The curve's instability across decades is not an embarrassment to
    econometrics; it is the field's clearest demonstration that estimated relationships
    are conditional on the regime that generated the data. Modern estimates therefore
    condition on expectations — anchored or de-anchored — before saying anything
    quantitative about the curve's slope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "adding more control variables fixes causality."
    Controls help only if they block genuine confounding paths; controlling for a
    variable that is itself an outcome of the policy (or a collider) can inject bias
    that was not there. Identification comes from design — a comparison that plausibly
    holds the counterfactual fixed — and no quantity of regressors substitutes for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the evaluation problem in one sentence, and explain why a naive before-and-after comparison fails as an answer to it.</li>
    <li>A jobs-programme region's employment moves from 100 to 104 while an untreated comparison region moves from 100 to 99. What does difference-in-differences credit the programme with, and why is that the better number?</li>
    <li>Why must an instrumental variable be tested for a strong first stage, and what did the Vietnam-draft lottery instrument have that most proposed instruments lack?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) After a policy we observe the economy with the policy but
    never the same economy without it; before-and-after fails because everything else was
    changing too — recessions end, oil prices move — so the comparison mixes the policy's effect
    with the business cycle. (2) The difference-in-differences estimate is (+4) − (−1) = +5: the
    control region's decline reveals the counterfactual path the treated region would have
    followed anyway, which neither the before-and-after view (+4) nor the snapshot comparison
    (5) captures correctly on its own. (3) A weak instrument biases two-stage estimates toward
    OLS with deceptively small standard errors, so the first stage must be tested — the
    traditional warning sign is an F-statistic under 10; the draft lottery was random and
    strongly affected service, delivering relevance and exogeneity together, which most proposed
    instruments cannot demonstrate.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Econometrics is statistics under economics' constraints: observational data, no chosen treatment, causal questions.</li>
    <li>The evaluation problem is the core: you never observe the same economy without the policy.</li>
    <li>Difference-in-differences reads the treated group's change against a control group's change — and stands or falls on parallel pre-trends.</li>
    <li>Multiplier evidence (narrative, cross-state, the crisis self-audit) varies with slack and the monetary response; the variation is the finding, not noise.</li>
    <li>Spurious regression warns that trending series need differencing or cointegration, not trust.</li>
    <li>Growth accounting leaves the Solow residual as an honest measure of what the data cannot explain.</li>
    <li>Design beats statistics: a clean natural experiment with simple methods beats a clever model on messy data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The two sibling expert lessons pick up the two halves of this chapter. <em>Macroeconomic
    Models: DSGE</em> builds the structural models that turn estimated relationships into
    counterfactual policy analysis, and <em>Financial Economics</em> applies the same
    identification discipline to markets, where asset prices in tiny event windows become the
    measuring instrument.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Instrumental variables and their weakness.</strong> The draft lottery
    instrument (Angrist, 1990) worked because lottery numbers were random and strongly
    affected service. Most proposed instruments are not so kind: a weak instrument
    (first-stage F-statistic under 10 is the traditional warning) makes two-stage
    estimates biased toward OLS and gives misleadingly small standard errors. The craft
    is testing the first stage before trusting the second.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Regression discontinuity.</strong> When treatment turns on a threshold — a
    scholarship just missed, a class-size cap just crossed — units immediately either
    side are comparable by construction, and the discontinuity estimates a local causal
    effect with unusual credibility. Panel methods, discontinuities and
    difference-in-differences now dominate applied micro-macro work alike; each carries
    its own diagnostic battery (manipulation tests, pre-trends, placebo thresholds).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Machine learning meets econometrics.</strong> The frontier is
    <em>double machine learning</em> (Chernozhukov and co-authors, 2018): use flexible
    learners to model the nuisance relationships, then recover the causal parameter with
    honest inference — plus causal forests that estimate heterogeneous effects instead
    of one average slope. Meanwhile the field polices itself: replication archives,
    pre-analysis plans, and specification-curve plots that show how many defensible
    answers a dataset contains. The credibility revolution's next act is making the
    researcher's degrees of freedom visible — and the unresolved question beyond all of
    it is external validity: an estimate earned in one natural experiment travels only
    as far as the analogy behind it.
  </p>

